Use valence-bond and hybrid-orbital models with an explicit purpose (@gc-vb: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94", #source "../sources/MIT-5-112.lingua#r_05VDAXGTY0M9E5K9TMJTWTFNAG", #source "../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ", #general-chemistry "Molecular-orbitals-and-extended-electronic-states.lingua#r_726ETM9745704A826Y42QSVD0T") { r_3GQ3A9GZAMRV47GHHDVDPRNZCT
**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 8’s valence-bond and hybrid-orbital material; [Principles of Chemical Science — MIT 5.112, Fall 2005](../sources/MIT-5-112.lingua#r_05VDAXGTY0M9E5K9TMJTWTFNAG), lectures 24–25 and the corresponding course notes; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), the bonding tutorial’s orbital treatments.

Valence-bond descriptions connect localized bonding with overlap and electron pairing under a chosen approximation. Hybrid orbitals are combinations of basis functions useful for representing particular local arrangements. Relate them to geometry and bonding rather than treating a hybridization label as an independently measured physical mechanism.

Distinguish sigma and pi interactions, localized and delocalized descriptions, and changes associated with rotation or conjugation. The model can organize patterns and reactivity, while quantitative energies and magnetic/electronic properties may require a more extensive treatment. Several descriptions can be useful for different questions about the same species.

**Work:** use a simple carbon compound and an unsaturated example to relate geometry, basis functions, and sigma/pi bonding. Explain what information the construction preserves and which electronic behavior it does not predict. Compare the textbook presentation with MIT 5.112's valence-bond lecture and identify a claim that needs another model or experimental evidence.

Read the full advanced-bonding chapter and work its exercises, not only the diagrams. Draw orbital signs and orientation where they matter to overlap. State why a particular basis is convenient for the question, and avoid using a naming mnemonic as the entire explanation of measured structure.

The next Record develops molecular orbitals and a route toward extended solids. Quantum mechanics and theoretical chemistry will supply the deeper mathematical foundations; this general chemistry treatment should already let a reader compare complementary models and understand their empirical consequences.

**Continue:** [Connect molecular orbitals with magnetism and extended electronic states](Molecular-orbitals-and-extended-electronic-states.lingua#r_726ETM9745704A826Y42QSVD0T).

